# A name the body binds by its own code holds the value the body made there, in its frame, from the binding on (#237,
# #355), and a change in place makes it anew. A choice's value is what its arms make of it: what one arm makes anew of a
# value named before the choice, which the other arm keeps, is one value at the choice's moment, as it is of a value a step
# made (#366). `picked` binds an empty list and appends the level to it where it is loud; `gathered` binds a list of the
# first level, reading it there, and appends the second where it is loud. In both the append takes the body's own list,
# which stands in the route's frame, and the step after the choice takes the choice's value. `loud` is of no type of the
# code's, and the route's own parameters of no type hold none. The plan is the Rust program's (`rust.plan`).

route levels.picked
choice picked/?1                                       # `if loud:`
step picked/?1/append
step picked/_show

line entry > picked call
line Level·level > picked value                        # the route's own parameter, from its start
line Level·both@picked/~both > picked/?1/append value  # `both: list[Level] = []`, the body's own
line Level·level > picked/?1/append value              # `both.append(level)`
line picked/?1/append > Level·both@picked/?1 value     # made anew in one arm, kept in the other
line Level·both@picked/?1 > picked/_show value         # `_show(both)`
line picked/_show > Screen own

home Level·level picked
home Level·both@picked/~both picked
home Level·both@picked/?1 picked

side entry left
side Screen right

route levels.gathered
choice gathered/?1
step gathered/?1/append
step gathered/_show

line entry > gathered call
line Level·first > gathered value                      # `both: list[Level] = [first]`, read where it is bound
line Level·second > gathered value
line Level·both@gathered/~both > gathered/?1/append value
line Level·second > gathered/?1/append value           # `both.append(second)`
line gathered/?1/append > Level·both@gathered/?1 value
line Level·both@gathered/?1 > gathered/_show value
line gathered/_show > Screen own

home Level·first gathered
home Level·second gathered
home Level·both@gathered/~both gathered
home Level·both@gathered/?1 gathered

side entry left
side Screen right

# The same rule in a loop: the list the body binds is the name's value for every step after the binding, in the loop as
# much as straight on, never the latest level the loop gives. A loop is a step: it takes what it goes over and gives each
# element under the name it binds. The append takes the body's list and the element and makes the list anew; the step
# after the loop takes what the append made.

route levels.looped
step looped/for level in levels
step looped/append
step looped/_show

line entry > looped call
line Level·levels > looped value
line Level·levels > looped/for level in levels value
line looped/for level in levels > Level·level@looped/for level in levels value
line Level·every@looped/~every > looped/append value   # `every: list[Level] = []`
line Level·level@looped/for level in levels > looped/append value
line looped/append > Level·every@looped/append value
line Level·every@looped/append > looped/_show value
line looped/_show > Screen own

home Level·levels looped
home Level·level@looped/for level in levels looped
home Level·every@looped/~every looped
home Level·every@looped/append looped

side entry left
side Screen right
